Q1. What is Mockito?

Interview Ready Answer

Mockito is an open-source Java Mocking Framework used with JUnit for
Unit Testing.

It creates mock (fake) objects to replace real dependencies such as
databases, repositories, REST APIs, or external services, allowing
classes to be tested independently.

Key Points

• Java Mocking Framework.
• Used with JUnit.
• Creates fake (mock) objects.
• Enables isolated Unit Testing.
• Eliminates dependency on external systems.

======================================================================

Q2. Why do we need Mockito?

Interview Ready Answer

Mockito is used to isolate the class under test by replacing external
dependencies with mock objects.

This makes unit tests faster, independent, reliable, and easier to
execute because they do not require real databases, APIs, or network
connections.

Advantages

• Fast execution.
• No database required.
• No external API required.
• Independent Unit Testing.
• Easier debugging.
• Reliable test execution.

Real-Life Example

UserService

↓

UserRepository

↓

Database

Instead of connecting to the real database,

Mockito creates a Mock Repository that returns predefined data.

======================================================================

Q3. What is Mocking?

Interview Ready Answer

Mocking is the process of creating fake objects that replace real
dependencies during Unit Testing.

These mock objects simulate the behavior of real objects without
performing actual operations.

Example

Real Database

↓

Mock Database

No real database connection is established.

======================================================================

Q4. What is Stubbing?

Interview Ready Answer

Stubbing is the process of defining the behavior of a mock object.

It specifies what value or response the mock object should return
when one of its methods is called.

Example

Mock Repository

↓

findUser(1)

↓

Return "Vishal"

No database is accessed.

======================================================================

Q5. Difference between Mocking and Stubbing.

Interview Ready Answer

Mocking

• Creates a fake object.
• Replaces a real dependency.
• First step in Mockito.

------------------------------------------------------------

Stubbing

• Defines the behavior of the mock object.
• Specifies predefined return values.
• Performed after creating the mock.

Memory Trick

Mock

↓

Create Fake Object

Stub

↓

Teach Fake Object
what to return.

======================================================================

Q6. What is verify() in Mockito?

Interview Ready Answer

verify() is a Mockito method used to verify whether a particular
method on a mock object was invoked during test execution.

It can also verify how many times that method was called.

Purpose

• Verify method invocation.
• Verify interaction with dependencies.
• Verify invocation count.

======================================================================

Q7. What is @Mock?

Interview Ready Answer

@Mock is a Mockito annotation used to create a mock (fake) object.

It replaces a real dependency such as a Repository or Service with
a fake implementation during Unit Testing.

======================================================================

Q8. What is @InjectMocks?

Interview Ready Answer

@InjectMocks is a Mockito annotation that injects all created mock
objects into the class being tested.

It allows the class under test to use mock dependencies instead of
real implementations.

======================================================================

Q9. What is @MockBean?

Interview Ready Answer

@MockBean is a Spring Boot testing annotation that replaces a real
Spring Bean with a mock object inside the Spring Application Context.

It is mainly used during Spring Boot Testing.

======================================================================

Q10. Difference between @Mock, @InjectMocks and @MockBean.

Interview Ready Answer

@Mock

• Creates a Mockito mock object.
• Used in Unit Testing.
• Does not load Spring Context.

------------------------------------------------------------

@InjectMocks

• Injects mock objects into the class under test.
• Used together with @Mock.

------------------------------------------------------------

@MockBean

• Creates a mock Spring Bean.
• Replaces a real bean in Spring Application Context.
• Used in Spring Boot Tests.

